  <h1>Low-Latency C++ for Trading</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Why this matters for <strong>HFT_practice</strong>:
    <code>gradient_boosting/sample_with_gradient_boosting.cpp</code> already shows
    the shape of a hot loop: per tick it computes a signal and reacts. Its own
    comment on line 62 admits the moving-average step is O(window) when it could
    be O(1). That single line is the entire low-latency C++ mindset — and once
    you spot it, you spot it everywhere.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §1 HOT-PATH RULES ===================== -->
  <h2>1. The hot-path rules — what you don't do inside a per-tick loop</h2>
  <p>
    A "hot path" is the code that runs once per market event. In a real HFT system
    that's millions of times a second. The rule is brutal: <strong>everything
    inside the hot loop must be predictable, allocation-free, and side-effect-free
    apart from the trade decision itself.</strong>
  </p>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>No heap allocation</h4>
      <p>No <code>new</code>, no <code>malloc</code>, no <code>std::vector::push_back</code> that might grow. Allocators take an unpredictable lock and can call the OS.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>No I/O</h4>
      <p><code>std::cout</code>, <code>printf</code>, <code>fprintf(stderr, ...)</code> — all forbidden. Each call locks a stream, formats, and may syscall.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>No syscalls</h4>
      <p>No <code>read</code>, <code>write</code>, <code>gettimeofday</code> outside of fast user-space variants. A syscall = a context switch = 100s of ns minimum.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>No exceptions</h4>
      <p>The throw path is enormously expensive and unpredictable. HFT code is usually compiled <code>-fno-exceptions</code> with explicit error codes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>No virtual calls</h4>
      <p>Indirect calls through a vtable kill inlining and cost a memory dereference. Use templates and CRTP for compile-time polymorphism.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>No locks</h4>
      <p>If two threads must share data, use lock-free queues (SPSC ring buffers) and atomics. <code>std::mutex</code> in the hot path is a panic button.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Mental model:</strong> the hot loop is closer to embedded firmware than
    to "normal" C++. Pre-allocate everything before the loop; touch only those
    arenas during it; report or log only after a batch of ticks.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §2 LATENCY COSTS ===================== -->
  <h2>2. What things actually cost (on a modern Linux server)</h2>
  <p>
    Memorize this table. Every "is this fast enough?" question reduces to looking
    up entries in it.
  </p>

  <div class="lat-cost">
    <div class="lat-row fast"><span class="lat-name">Register read / ALU op</span><span>add, sub, integer multiply</span><span class="lat-num">~0.3 ns</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row fast"><span class="lat-name">L1 cache hit</span><span>data already hot</span><span class="lat-num">~1 ns</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row fast"><span class="lat-name">L2 / L3 cache hit</span><span>data on this core / socket</span><span class="lat-num">~3–15 ns</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row fast"><span class="lat-name">Branch mispredict</span><span>pipeline flush + refill</span><span class="lat-num">~10–20 ns</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row medium"><span class="lat-name">Main memory (RAM) read</span><span>cache miss</span><span class="lat-num">~100 ns</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row medium"><span class="lat-name">Spinlock acquire (uncontended)</span><span>atomic CAS</span><span class="lat-num">~25 ns</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row medium"><span class="lat-name">Heap allocation</span><span><code>malloc</code> small block, glibc</span><span class="lat-num">~30–200 ns</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row medium"><span class="lat-name">Virtual function call</span><span>vtable load + indirect jmp</span><span class="lat-num">~5–15 ns + missed inlining</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row slow"><span class="lat-name">Lightweight syscall (e.g. <code>read</code>)</span><span>kernel entry/exit</span><span class="lat-num">~300 ns – 1 µs</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row slow"><span class="lat-name">Mutex lock under contention</span><span>kernel may sleep the thread</span><span class="lat-num">~1–10 µs</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row slow"><span class="lat-name"><code>std::cout</code> one line, ~30 chars</span><span>iostream lock + format + write</span><span class="lat-num">~1–5 µs</span></div>
    <div class="lat-row slow"><span class="lat-name">Exception throw + catch</span><span>stack unwind</span><span class="lat-num">~1–10 µs</span></div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Compare to your FPGA's ~4 ns clock cycle (Lesson 0002). A single
    <code>std::cout</code> line in the hot path of a C++ trading loop is roughly
    <strong>1000 FPGA cycles</strong>. That's the ratio you're fighting.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §3 STACK/HEAP/CACHE ===================== -->
  <h2>3. Stack, heap, and cache — the three things you allocate from</h2>

  <h3>Stack: free, fast, fixed size</h3>
<pre><span class="ty">double</span> prices[<span class="num">256</span>];                <span class="com">// Stack: instant, no allocator. Fixed size.</span>
std::<span class="ty">array</span>&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>, <span class="num">256</span>&gt; prices;     <span class="com">// Same, but typed and bounds-checked in debug.</span>
</pre>
  <p>
    The stack is just a pointer move. Allocation is "free" — no calls, no locks,
    no fragmentation. The catch: size must be known at compile time, and large
    stack frames can overflow.
  </p>

  <h3>Heap: flexible, expensive, unpredictable</h3>
<pre>std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt; prices;          <span class="com">// Heap-backed; capacity grows as you push_back.</span>
prices.<span class="fn">push_back</span>(p);                <span class="com">// May trigger malloc + memcpy under the hood.</span>
</pre>
  <p>
    Every <code>push_back</code> past current capacity triggers a heap allocation
    and a copy of all existing elements. In the hot path, that single line can
    cost hundreds of nanoseconds — non-deterministically.
  </p>

  <p>The HFT-grade alternative: a <strong>ring buffer</strong> on the stack.</p>

<pre><span class="kw">template</span>&lt;<span class="kw">typename</span> T, std::<span class="ty">size_t</span> N&gt;
<span class="kw">class</span> RingBuffer {
    std::<span class="ty">array</span>&lt;T, N&gt; data_{};
    std::<span class="ty">size_t</span>        head_ = <span class="num">0</span>;       <span class="com">// Next write slot</span>
    std::<span class="ty">size_t</span>        count_ = <span class="num">0</span>;      <span class="com">// How many filled, capped at N</span>
<span class="kw">public</span>:
    <span class="fn">void</span> <span class="fn">push</span>(T v) <span class="kw">noexcept</span> {
        data_[head_] = v;
        head_ = (head_ + <span class="num">1</span>) % N;            <span class="com">// O(1), no allocation, ever</span>
        <span class="kw">if</span> (count_ &lt; N) ++count_;
    }
    T <span class="fn">oldest</span>() <span class="kw">const</span> <span class="kw">noexcept</span> {              <span class="com">// O(1) accessor for the FIFO front</span>
        <span class="kw">return</span> data_[(head_ + N - count_) % N];
    }
};
</pre>

  <p>
    Same shape as the VHDL <code>price_reg</code> array in
    <code>fpga/moving_average_trader.vhd</code> — fixed-size FIFO with O(1) updates.
    The hardware and the C++ converge for exactly the same reasons.
  </p>

  <h3>Cache: where the data <em>actually</em> lives</h3>
  <p>
    A "memory read" that misses L1 costs ~100 ns. A read that hits L1 costs ~1 ns.
    Hot-path C++ is largely an exercise in keeping working sets <strong>cache-resident</strong>.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cache line = 64 bytes.</strong> Reading one byte fetches the whole line.</li>
    <li><strong>Sequential access is fast</strong> because the hardware prefetches the next line. <code>std::vector</code> wins big here; linked lists lose horribly.</li>
    <li><strong>Struct-of-Arrays beats Array-of-Structs</strong> when you process one field at a time across many rows (vectorization-friendly).</li>
    <li><strong>False sharing</strong>: two threads writing different variables that happen to share a cache line will ping-pong the line between caches. Pad to 64 bytes (<code>alignas(64)</code>) to avoid it.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- ===================== §4 BRANCH & INLINE ===================== -->
  <h2>4. Branch prediction, inlining, and the cost of being wrong</h2>

  <h3>Branches</h3>
  <p>
    The CPU pipeline is ~15 stages deep. To keep it full, the front-end
    <em>predicts</em> the outcome of every conditional branch before it knows the
    answer. When the prediction is right (the common case in a well-trained
    predictor), branches are nearly free. When it's wrong, the pipeline flushes
    and refills — ~10–20 ns wasted.
  </p>
  <p>
    In <code>sample_with_gradient_boosting.cpp</code>:
  </p>
<pre><span class="kw">if</span> (price &lt; movingAvg - SPREAD) { ... }
<span class="kw">else if</span> (price &gt; movingAvg + SPREAD &amp;&amp; position &gt; <span class="num">0</span>) { ... }
<span class="com">// otherwise, hold position — the common case</span>
</pre>
  <p>
    Most ticks fall into the "hold" branch (the dead-band). The compiler/predictor
    will learn this. But you can make it explicit:
  </p>
<pre><span class="kw">if</span> (price &lt; movingAvg - SPREAD) [[<span class="kw">unlikely</span>]] {       <span class="com">// C++20</span>
    <span class="com">// rare buy path</span>
} <span class="kw">else if</span> (price &gt; movingAvg + SPREAD &amp;&amp; position &gt; <span class="num">0</span>) [[<span class="kw">unlikely</span>]] {
    <span class="com">// rare sell path</span>
}
<span class="com">// implicit common "hold" — predicted not-taken, no mispredict</span>
</pre>
  <p>
    <code>[[likely]]</code> and <code>[[unlikely]]</code> hint to the compiler
    about which branch to keep on the fall-through (hot) path. They don't replace
    the hardware predictor; they help the compiler lay out code so the cold paths
    sit far away and don't pollute the icache.
  </p>

  <h3>Inlining and the case against virtual calls</h3>
  <p>
    A function call is ~5 ns at minimum (call/ret + register spilling). Inlining
    removes that overhead and lets the compiler optimize across the boundary.
    Templates are aggressively inlined; <code>virtual</code> calls cannot be inlined
    in general because the target isn't known until runtime.
  </p>

  <p>
    HFT C++ replaces dynamic polymorphism with <strong>static polymorphism via
    templates</strong> or the Curiously Recurring Template Pattern (CRTP):
  </p>

<pre><span class="kw">template</span>&lt;<span class="kw">class</span> Strategy&gt;
<span class="fn">void</span> <span class="fn">runTick</span>(Strategy&amp; s, <span class="ty">double</span> price) {
    s.<span class="fn">onTick</span>(price);    <span class="com">// Direct call, fully inlinable, no vtable</span>
}

<span class="kw">struct</span> MeanReversion {
    <span class="fn">void</span> <span class="fn">onTick</span>(<span class="ty">double</span> p) <span class="kw">noexcept</span> { <span class="com">/* … */</span> }
};

<span class="fn">runTick</span>(mr_strategy, p);   <span class="com">// Compiler sees MeanReversion::onTick directly.</span>
</pre>

  <h3>The <code>noexcept</code> contract</h3>
  <p>
    Marking a function <code>noexcept</code> tells the compiler "this can't throw"
    — which lets it skip generating the exception-unwinding scaffolding and inline
    more aggressively. Use it on every leaf function in the hot path.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §5 IN THIS PROJECT ===================== -->
  <h2>5. In this project — rewriting <code>sample_with_gradient_boosting.cpp</code> for HFT</h2>

  <h3>The original (lines 69–82, lightly trimmed)</h3>
  <span class="filepath">gradient_boosting/sample_with_gradient_boosting.cpp</span>
<pre><span class="ty">double</span> <span class="fn">calculateMovingAverage</span>(<span class="kw">const</span> std::vector&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt;&amp; prices, <span class="ty">int</span> window) {
    <span class="kw">if</span> (<span class="kw">static_cast</span>&lt;<span class="ty">int</span>&gt;(prices.<span class="fn">size</span>()) &lt; window) {
        <span class="kw">return</span> prices.<span class="fn">back</span>();
    }
    <span class="ty">double</span> sum = <span class="num">0.0</span>;
    <span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="ty">int</span> i = <span class="kw">static_cast</span>&lt;<span class="ty">int</span>&gt;(prices.<span class="fn">size</span>()) - window;
         i &lt; <span class="kw">static_cast</span>&lt;<span class="ty">int</span>&gt;(prices.<span class="fn">size</span>()); ++i) {
        sum += prices[i];
    }
    <span class="kw">return</span> sum / <span class="kw">static_cast</span>&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>&gt;(window);
}
</pre>
  <p>What's costly here, line by line:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Argument <code>const std::vector&amp;</code></strong>: vector header lives on the heap; member access is a pointer chase. <em>Mostly</em> fine because of caching, but the API ties this function to a heap container.</li>
    <li><strong>Loop body</strong>: 20 additions and 1 division per call. Now multiply by 1000 ticks = 20,000 adds. The lesson 0002 VHDL does this in <em>one add and one subtract per tick, period</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Caller's <code>prices.push_back(price)</code></strong>: heap reallocation when capacity is exceeded — non-deterministic stall.</li>
    <li><strong>Caller's <code>std::cout &lt;&lt; "BUY at" &lt;&lt; …</code></strong>: ~1–5 µs per executed trade line. Lethal at high tick rates.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The HFT-grade rewrite (sketch)</h3>
<pre><span class="kw">template</span>&lt;std::<span class="ty">size_t</span> WINDOW&gt;
<span class="kw">class</span> MeanReversion {
    std::<span class="ty">array</span>&lt;<span class="ty">double</span>, WINDOW&gt; ring_{};   <span class="com">// stack-allocated ring buffer</span>
    std::<span class="ty">size_t</span>                    head_ = <span class="num">0</span>;
    std::<span class="ty">size_t</span>                    count_ = <span class="num">0</span>;
    <span class="ty">double</span>                         sum_ = <span class="num">0.0</span>;    <span class="com">// running sum, like the VHDL</span>
    <span class="kw">static constexpr</span> <span class="ty">double</span> SPREAD = <span class="num">0.05</span>;        <span class="com">// compile-time constant</span>

<span class="kw">public</span>:
    <span class="kw">enum class</span> Action : <span class="ty">uint8_t</span> { Hold = <span class="num">0</span>, Buy = <span class="num">1</span>, Sell = <span class="num">2</span> };

    Action <span class="fn">onTick</span>(<span class="ty">double</span> price) <span class="kw">noexcept</span> {
        <span class="com">// 1. Incremental sum update: O(1) regardless of WINDOW.</span>
        <span class="kw">if</span> (count_ == WINDOW) {
            sum_ -= ring_[head_];        <span class="com">// drop the oldest</span>
        } <span class="kw">else</span> {
            ++count_;                    <span class="com">// warm-up</span>
        }
        ring_[head_] = price;
        sum_ += price;
        head_ = (head_ + <span class="num">1</span>) % WINDOW;

        <span class="kw">if</span> (count_ &lt; WINDOW) [[<span class="kw">unlikely</span>]] <span class="kw">return</span> Action::Hold;

        <span class="kw">const</span> <span class="ty">double</span> avg = sum_ / WINDOW;
        <span class="kw">if</span> (price &lt; avg - SPREAD) [[<span class="kw">unlikely</span>]] <span class="kw">return</span> Action::Buy;
        <span class="kw">if</span> (price &gt; avg + SPREAD) [[<span class="kw">unlikely</span>]] <span class="kw">return</span> Action::Sell;
        <span class="kw">return</span> Action::Hold;
    }
};
</pre>
  <p>What we changed and why:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stack ring buffer</strong> instead of <code>std::vector</code>: no allocation, ever.</li>
    <li><strong>Running sum</strong>: O(1) update instead of O(WINDOW) recomputation — same trick as <code>fpga/moving_average_trader.vhd</code> from Lesson 0002.</li>
    <li><strong>Templated <code>WINDOW</code> + <code>constexpr SPREAD</code></strong>: both baked in at compile time. The compiler can unroll and constant-fold.</li>
    <li><strong><code>noexcept</code></strong>: no exception machinery generated.</li>
    <li><strong>Return value, not <code>std::cout</code></strong>: the I/O is moved out of the hot loop. The caller decides whether to log; in production, log to a pre-allocated ring of records and flush from a separate thread.</li>
    <li><strong><code>[[unlikely]]</code></strong>: signals the cold paths so the hold-case stays on the fall-through.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>What you don't have to change:</strong> the algorithm. Same mean-reversion
    rule, same parameters, same trades. All the gains come from the discipline of
    how the same math is expressed in the language. That's the whole HFT C++ skill.
  </div>

  <h3>And on the egress side: <code>gradient_boosting_data_fetching.cpp</code></h3>
  <span class="filepath">gradient_boosting/gradient_boosting_data_fetching.cpp (lines 15–22)</span>
<pre>std::<span class="ty">string</span> dataBuffer;                       <span class="com">// global, heap-backed, grows unboundedly</span>

<span class="ty">size_t</span> <span class="fn">WriteCallback</span>(<span class="fn">void</span>* contents, ... ) {
    <span class="ty">size_t</span> totalSize = size * nmemb;
    dataBuffer.<span class="fn">append</span>((<span class="ty">char</span>*)contents, totalSize);   <span class="com">// heap-allocates on growth</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> totalSize;
}
</pre>
  <p>
    cURL over HTTPS to Alpha Vantage is a <em>cold path</em> — it polls a REST API
    once per minute. So this file is fine as written; the unbounded heap append is
    a future cleanup, not an HFT crime. The lesson is to know which path you're on:
    you allocate freely on the cold path and never on the hot path.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §6 CONTRASTS ===================== -->
  <h2>6. Often confused with</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4><code>std::array</code> vs <code>std::vector</code></h4>
      <p>
        <strong><code>std::array&lt;T, N&gt;</code></strong>: fixed size, stack-allocated, no
        allocator. Use everywhere in the hot path.
        <strong><code>std::vector&lt;T&gt;</code></strong>: dynamic size, heap-backed. Use
        only when size genuinely varies and only outside hot loops.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Latency vs Throughput</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Latency</strong>: time for one operation. HFT obsesses about this.
        <strong>Throughput</strong>: operations per second. Data engineering
        obsesses about this. They often trade off; busy-polling burns CPU
        (lowers throughput) to cut latency.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Cache vs Main memory</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Cache</strong> (L1/L2/L3): on-die SRAM, 1–15 ns access. Holds recently
        used lines automatically. <strong>Main memory</strong>: off-die DRAM, ~100 ns.
        The 100× factor is what makes "is the data hot in cache" the most important
        question in performance work.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Inlining vs Calling</h4>
      <p>
        An <strong>inlined</strong> function has its body pasted at the call site —
        no call/ret, full cross-function optimization. A <strong>called</strong>
        function (especially <code>virtual</code>) is an opaque jump that blocks
        optimization. Templates inline; virtuals don't.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Branch prediction vs Branch elimination</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Prediction</strong>: the CPU guesses the outcome. Cheap when right.
        <strong>Elimination</strong>: write the code so there's no branch at all
        (e.g. <code>x = cond * a + (1 - cond) * b;</code>). When the branch is
        unpredictable, eliminating it can win even with extra arithmetic.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Mutex vs Atomic</h4>
      <p>
        A <strong>mutex</strong> blocks the thread on contention; the kernel may
        sleep it (1–10 µs). An <strong>atomic</strong> operation is a single
        instruction (CAS, LOCK XADD) costing ~25 ns. Lock-free queues use
        atomics to communicate between threads without locks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

