  <!-- ===================== §8 SCENARIOS ===================== -->
  <h2>8. Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S1. A profiler reports that 40% of CPU time in your hot loop is in <code>operator new</code>. Without seeing the code, name three plausible culprits and the corresponding fix.</summary>
    <p>
      Three usual suspects: (a) a <code>std::vector::push_back</code> growing past
      capacity each loop — pre-<code>reserve()</code> or switch to a stack
      <code>std::array</code> / ring buffer; (b) a <code>std::string</code> built per
      iteration for logging — replace with a fixed-size <code>char[64]</code> or a
      lock-free record ring; (c) a <code>std::unique_ptr</code> or
      <code>std::shared_ptr</code> created per tick to wrap some "event" — drop
      the pointer and pass the object by value or by reference into a pre-allocated
      buffer. The general fix is the same in all three: move allocation out of the
      loop, keep the loop allocator-free.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S2. You add <code>[[unlikely]]</code> to a branch and benchmark — no improvement. Why might this be expected?</summary>
    <p>
      Two common reasons. First, modern CPU branch predictors are very good; if
      your branch is well-predicted by the hardware already, the hint is redundant.
      Second, <code>[[unlikely]]</code> mainly affects code layout (keeping cold
      paths far from the hot path), so the win shows up in instruction cache
      pressure — visible only when you have a complex hot loop or measure under
      realistic working-set conditions. On a tight microbenchmark with one branch,
      the hint can be a no-op. The right discipline: use the hints, but never
      ship a "fix" without a measurement against the real workload.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S3. The repo's <code>main.cpp</code> uses ONNX Runtime to load a gradient-boosting model. Roughly how does ONNX inference fit into the latency budget, and what would a "moving the model to the hot path" decision look like?</summary>
    <p>
      ONNX Runtime inference for a small GBM is typically in the 1–10 µs range
      on a CPU — orders of magnitude slower than the per-tick arithmetic in
      <code>onTick</code>, but still fast enough to gate a decision once per
      <em>second</em> or even per <em>0.1 s</em>. The decision tree to move the
      model into the hot path is the same as Lesson 0007's hls4ml conversation:
      either compile a tiny quantized model into HDL on the FPGA (target ns
      latency), or keep ONNX as a cold-path signal that updates parameters
      consumed by a fast hand-rolled hot loop (target µs latency). Don't try
      to put ONNX Runtime in the per-tick loop verbatim; the right pattern is
      <em>fast hot loop reads parameters that a slow path occasionally refreshes</em>.
    </p>
  </details>

